-    "tests_readers_three_bucket": "that the COST of the book is right — it proves only that three read-only screens (daily_summary per-anchor column, first_anchor_review §3c, score_post_fix E6) now classify every fill as measured / fee-unknown / unpriced under the SAME rule as anchor_loop.neutrality_price (asserted equal key by key on shared fixtures), print a bps over the measured set only, print None with the bucket counts when nothing was measured, count an unknown fill apart from a zero fill, and that the pre-fix formulas give a number on those same rows (2026-08-05 12:00Z real anchor: 103/103 fee-unknown, old -3.70bps). Four blind spots: (a) it does not convert a non-USDT fee — a BNB commission stays UNKNOWN, so a screen can now say less than before, never more; (b) pilot_metrics.m1 (the §4-1 caliber) still keeps fee-unknown notional in its denominator at fee 0 and reads complete beside a NaN fee; it is FROZEN (check_metrics_freeze) and not changed here, so E6 derives its completeness bit from the buckets, carries m1's bit beside it with the disagreement flagged, and keeps m1's c_bps_overall as the pre-registered figure; (c) the equality assertion pins the copy in cost_buckets to neutrality_price, not to chase_readout, whose collect() needs a whole tree — the two production diagnostics are assumed to agree because the review rounds asserted it; (d) the real-ledger checks read the state copy at whatever age it is, and go NOT EXERCISED (not green) when the 2026-08-05 day file is absent. Round 3 (2026-09-13, researcher REVIEW §3.C): E6 may PASS only when its verdict's population and the number it publishes come from the SAME rows — the bucket counts and notional mass against m1's own `n_filled_orders`/`filled_notional_total`, pooled AND per regime, plus agreement of both bps calibers inside a stated tolerance — and the screen now publishes the bucket figure (`cost_bps_displayed`) beside m1's. Blind spots this adds: (e) the population identity is asserted on COUNTS AND MASS each carrier reports for itself, not on row identities, so two different row sets agreeing in count and notional (pooled and in every regime) would still pass; (f) the gate makes E6 refuse to certify a mismatch — it does not FIX m1, which stays frozen, so the number the pre-registration names (`c_bps_overall`) is still m1's; (g) the frozen `watchdog` §4-1 consumer still reads `m1.c_bps_net_overall` directly and treats a NaN cost as priced and not a breach, so 'all consumers are on the three buckets' is FALSE and must not be written anywhere. Round 4 (2026-09-13): first_anchor_review reads every numeric field it prints — readback notional and quantity, equity, target_gross, fill amount, intended notional, exchange floor filters, anchor mid — as a finite number or NOT OBSERVABLE, with a finite 0 kept as a value; a NaN readback notional or a NaN equity used to print the band '★★ ABOVE 15%' on the stop-decision block, and pre-fix readers fail 11 of the [D-R4] cells. Blind spots it adds: (h) the [D-R4] trees write NaN through the validating writer but None only where the schema allows it, so a None in a not_null column (nav, target_gross, venue_position_notional) is covered by the shared finite-number reader, not by a fixture row; (i) target_w keeps its is-not-None skip — a NaN weight prints a visible nan — registered, not changed",
+    "tests_daily_summary": "that the operator will UNDERSTAND the summary — it proves the three questions are answered and that the day-boundary residual refuses rather than computes, not that the explanations land. Four blind spots: (a) most checks are substring assertions over the rendered text, so rewording a line silently breaks the guarantee while the tool still runs; (b) it verifies the numbers are PRESENT, never that they are RIGHT — a wrong wallet balance formatted correctly passes every check here; (c) the external-flow line says money is 'probably in spot' when TRANSFER is zero, which is an inference we cannot verify because the system has no spot-wallet access at all; (d) it runs on whatever the ledger currently holds, so a day with no rotation exercises none of the Q2 path and still goes green Its Q2/day-boundary prose checks are conditional on the nav-row count the tool itself reports for its `--since` window (round 5, 2026-09-10): on a state copy older than 24h they print NOT EXERCISED rather than pass or fail — so a green run on a stale copy proves nothing about those two lines. W2 2026-09-12: `account_facts` now reports realised P&L as a RECORD (USDT-only slice; non-USDT amounts listed UNCONVERTED, never summed; `realised_pnl=None` ⇒ not observable and the same-day residual refuses; a truncated income read is labelled incomplete; a pre-split row is passed through under a STATED assumption) and the per-anchor cost column uses the three buckets — both asserted on fields with the reviewer's two counterexamples as inline mutations and on real ledger rows; it still cannot CONVERT a foreign-asset fee, only name it. Round 3 (2026-09-13, researcher REVIEW §3.C): the same-day residual's flow term is now the INTERVAL difference of the day-cumulative `external_flow_usdt` (the old form subtracted a day total from an interval equity change and read -1000 on a window where nothing happened) and refuses when either endpoint is not a finite number. Two further blind spots it does NOT close: (e) the interval form is exact only because the residual is computed same-day, where both endpoints share one 00:00Z origin — nothing here re-derives that the carrier really is cumulative, it is read from `binance_broker.income_since`; (f) a zero residual is not proof the account identity holds — it means the three terms we CAN read cancel, and any movement none of them carries (a fee type outside `realised_components`, a spot transfer) is invisible to it. Round 4 (2026-09-13, researcher round-3 monitoring NAV-R3-1): the equity endpoints (start and end nav, end wallet and unrealised) and each day's closing flow are read with the same three states as realised income — a finite 0 is a value, None / NaN / inf are not observable, and an endpoint is never replaced by 0 or by the other endpoint — so a start equity of 0 no longer turns into the end equity (-100 printed on a deposit into an empty account) and a NaN equity no longer marks the residual computable; an unknown maker intent refuses the fill-rate instead of shrinking its denominator. Pre-fix readers fail 29 of its round-4 cells. Two further blind spots: (g) these are CAPABILITY cells — the ledger copy (251 nav rows, 74,860 order rows) holds no None, NaN or zero equity, so they run on synthetic rows and two temp-ledger runs of main, never on a real day where it happened; (h) FIXED by E7 (FX-EXEC 2026-09-13): a window with no nav row renders the account as NOT OBSERVABLE and names the ledger's newest row as before the window and not used ([E7-a], the entry point on a temp ledger); the four [B3] account-text promises that were exercised only through the stale-row fallback (Q1, Q3, the sight boundary, the rotation line) are now NOT EXERCISED on a window without a nav row, like Q2, and E7-a4 asserts the NOT OBSERVABLE account in that case.",
+    "tests_readers_three_bucket": "that the COST of the book is right — it proves only that three read-only screens (daily_summary per-anchor column, first_anchor_review §3c, score_post_fix E6) now classify every fill as measured / fee-unknown / unpriced under the SAME rule as anchor_loop.neutrality_price (asserted equal key by key on shared fixtures), print a bps over the measured set only, print None with the bucket counts when nothing was measured, count an unknown fill apart from a zero fill, and that the pre-fix formulas give a number on those same rows (2026-08-05 12:00Z real anchor: 103/103 fee-unknown, old -3.70bps). Four blind spots: (a) it does not convert a non-USDT fee — a BNB commission stays UNKNOWN, so a screen can now say less than before, never more; (b) pilot_metrics.m1 (the §4-1 caliber) still keeps fee-unknown notional in its denominator at fee 0 and reads complete beside a NaN fee; it is FROZEN (check_metrics_freeze) and not changed here, so E6 derives its completeness bit from the buckets, carries m1's bit beside it with the disagreement flagged, and keeps m1's c_bps_overall as the pre-registered figure; (c) the equality assertion pins the copy in cost_buckets to neutrality_price, not to chase_readout, whose collect() needs a whole tree — the two production diagnostics are assumed to agree because the review rounds asserted it; (d) the real-ledger checks read the state copy at whatever age it is, and go NOT EXERCISED (not green) when the 2026-08-05 day file is absent. Round 3 (2026-09-13, researcher REVIEW §3.C): E6 may PASS only when its verdict's population and the number it publishes come from the SAME rows — the bucket counts and notional mass against m1's own `n_filled_orders`/`filled_notional_total`, pooled AND per regime, plus agreement of both bps calibers inside a stated tolerance — and the screen now publishes the bucket figure (`cost_bps_displayed`) beside m1's. Blind spots this adds: (e) the population identity is asserted on COUNT